2025-05-01 03:29:33 - Thread-1469 (execute) - INFO - [Exploration]
Relevant tables & columns for the task  
(payment behaviour of customers from one month to the next)

• payment.amount : money paid in every individual transaction – will be summed per month.  
• payment.payment_date : time‑stamp that lets us extract year‑month parts so we can compare consecutive months.  
• payment.customer_id : ties each payment row to a specific customer.  
• customer.customer_id : primary key, lets us join to payment.  
• customer.first_name / customer.last_name : give the readable customer name when we present the result.  

Typical conditions we will end up using  
• GROUP BY customer_id to get totals per customer.  
• ORDER BY total DESC LIMIT 10 to isolate the top‑10 paying customers.  
• strftime('%Y-%m', payment_date) to aggregate by calendar month.  
• self‑join or window functions to compare one month with the next for the same customer.  

Below are ten exploratory SELECT statements (all limited to 20 rows) that progressively build insight into the data.  
Each is prefixed with a short comment describing its purpose.

```sql
--1. Peek at the distinct customers that appear in the payment table
SELECT DISTINCT "customer_id"
FROM "payment"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--2. See some raw payment rows to understand the timestamp and amount formats
SELECT "payment_id", "customer_id", "amount", "payment_date"
FROM "payment"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--3. Check the range of payment amounts that actually occur
SELECT DISTINCT "amount"
FROM "payment"
ORDER BY "amount"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--4. Inspect how many payments each of the first few customers has made
SELECT "customer_id", COUNT(*) AS payments_made
FROM "payment"
GROUP BY "customer_id"
ORDER BY "customer_id"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--5. Look at total lifetime spend per customer (unordered sample)
SELECT "customer_id", ROUND(SUM("amount"),2) AS total_spent
FROM "payment"
GROUP BY "customer_id"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--6. Rank customers by total spend to confirm who the top spenders are
SELECT "customer_id", ROUND(SUM("amount"),2) AS total_spent
FROM "payment"
GROUP BY "customer_id"
ORDER BY total_spent DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--7. Show month‑level spending for one sample customer to verify month extraction works
SELECT strftime('%Y-%m', "payment_date") AS yr_month,
       ROUND(SUM("amount"),2) AS monthly_spend
FROM "payment"
WHERE "customer_id" = 1
GROUP BY yr_month
ORDER BY yr_month
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--8. Produce month‑level spending for every customer (small sample)
SELECT "customer_id",
       strftime('%Y-%m', "payment_date") AS yr_month,
       ROUND(SUM("amount"),2) AS monthly_spend
FROM "payment"
GROUP BY "customer_id", yr_month
ORDER BY "customer_id", yr_month
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--9. Retrieve the top‑10 customers by lifetime spend (their IDs only)
SELECT "customer_id"
FROM "payment"
GROUP BY "customer_id"
ORDER BY SUM("amount") DESC
LIMIT 10;
```

```sql
--10. For those top‑10 customers, list consecutive months and their spend so that a follow‑up query can compute the month‑over‑month difference
SELECT p."customer_id",
       strftime('%Y-%m', p."payment_date") AS yr_month,
       ROUND(SUM(p."amount"),2) AS monthly_spend
FROM "payment" AS p
WHERE p."customer_id" IN (
      SELECT "customer_id"
      FROM "payment"
      GROUP BY "customer_id"
      ORDER BY SUM("amount") DESC
      LIMIT 10)
GROUP BY p."customer_id", yr_month
ORDER BY p."customer_id", yr_month
LIMIT 20;
```

These queries gradually surface all the information we need; once month‑level spending per top customer is in hand, the final analytical step (not shown here) would calculate each customer’s difference between a month and the immediately following month, then pick the largest absolute value to answer the business question.
